---
import StateLawList from '../../components/StateLawList.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { format, t } from '../../i18n';

const asOf = '2026-10-26';
---

<Base title={`${t('en', 'nav.rights')}: state laws · ${t('en', 'site.name')}`} lang="en">
  <div class="legal-notices">
    <p class="legal-stamp">
      <a href="/rights/whats-changed">
        {format(t('en', 'legal.statusAsOf'), { date: asOf })} · {t('en', 'legal.whatsChanged')}
      </a>
    </p>
    <p class="legal-nla">
      <a href="/help/lawyers">{t('en', 'legal.notLegalAdvice')}</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <article>
    <h1>State laws: captive-audience bans</h1>
    <p><strong>12 states ban mandatory anti-union meetings. Several bans are being challenged in court. Some are on hold.</strong></p>
    <h2>What this means for you</h2>
    <p>In these states, your employer can't force you to sit through an anti-union meeting: AK, CA, CT, HI, IL, ME, MN, NJ, NY, OR, VT, WA.</p>
    <p>Employers have sued in several states (CA, IL, OR, CT), and some laws are on hold pending those cases [A44]. The list below shows each state's current status.</p>
    <h2>What to do now</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find your state below.</li>
      <li>Even where the ban is in force, <strong>prepare your coworkers anyway</strong>. Assume the meeting is coming. A ban doesn't stop the boss from trying other pressure.</li>
      <li>Check the as-of date. These cases move monthly.</li>
    </ol>
    <StateLawList locale="en" />
    <h2>Watch out</h2>
    <p>This page is generated from our state-law records and re-checked as the lawsuits move. See <a href="/rights/whats-changed">what's changed</a> for the federal picture.</p>
  </article>
</Base>